course: dictionary
lesson: 2435
title: Reading about daffodils
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A daffodil is a yellow spring flower with a trumpet-shaped centre. It grows from a bulb, which is the round part under the ground. Scientists use daffodils to show the parts of a flower, and poets write about them too.
explain: Read the words around a new word to find its meaning. «Do not put wild plants or flowers in your mouth; foxgloves, daffodils and buttercups are poisonous.» The warning tells you that poisonous means harmful, and it could make you ill.
explain: Some words sound alike but mean different things. Daffodils and onions both grow from bulbs, so the sentence «Daffodil bulbs look like onions but are poisonous» tells you why you must be careful with them.
fact: A daffodil is a yellow spring flower with a trumpet-shaped centre.
fact: Daffodils grow from bulbs, which are the round parts under the ground.
fact: «Poisonous» means harmful, and it could make you ill if you eat or swallow it.
fact: Daffodils are the national flower of Wales.
fact: Wordsworth wrote a poem about «a host of golden daffodils».
exercise: Where does a daffodil grow from? | A stem full of water | A seed inside a pod | *A bulb under the ground | Its roots in the sea
exercise: «Foxgloves, daffodils and buttercups are poisonous.» What does poisonous mean? | Safe to eat | Sweet-smelling | *Harmful, and could make you ill | Very tall
exercise: «Daffodils and snowdrops flower before the trees have leaves.» Which season are daffodils linked with? | Winter | Autumn | Summer | *Spring
test: Why should daffodil bulbs be kept apart from onions? | Bulbs taste sweet and should be eaten | Onions make daffodils grow taller | Onions are poisonous to people | *Daffodil bulbs look like onions but are poisonous
test: Which description matches a daffodil? | A small blue flower that grows in water | *A yellow spring flower with a trumpet-shaped centre | A tall tree with green leaves all year | A red flower with black spots
exam: Which word in «a host of golden daffodils» tells you the colour? | *golden | of | daffodils | host
